tyle=3D"--animation-duration: 400ms; --fade-animation-function: linear;"><p=
 data-path-to-node=3D"0">Francis=E2=80=99s proposal is a classic, textbook =
Y-Combinator/Silicon Valley cap table for a three-person founding team. It =
is mathematically clean and investor-friendly, but it completely fractures =
your strategy of maintaining high founder control.</p><p data-path-to-node=
=3D"1">He is essentially giving you an ultimatum: he wants a 20% equal-part=
ner stake, or he walks.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"2">Here is a breakdown o=
f exactly what his proposal means for OpenWorldTech, weighing the startup s=
tandard against the reality of your leverage.</p><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"3=
">The Breakdown of Francis's Math</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"4">He is work=
ing backward from a standard 10,000,000 share pool:</p><ul data-path-to-nod=
e=3D"5"><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"5,0,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"5,0,0" d=
ata-index-in-node=3D"0">You (CEO/Architect):</b> 30%</p></li><li><p data-pa=
th-to-node=3D"5,1,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"5,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0=
">Francis (COO):</b> 20%</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"5,2,0"><b data=
-path-to-node=3D"5,2,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Future CTO:</b> 20%</p></l=
i><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"5,3,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"5,3,0" data-in=
dex-in-node=3D"0">Investor/Option Sleeve:</b> 30%</p></li></ul><h3 data-pat=
h-to-node=3D"6">The Strategic Upside</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"7">Francis=
 isn't trying to pull a fast one; this is exactly how accelerators teach fo=
unders to slice the pie to prevent future headaches.</p><ul data-path-to-no=
de=3D"8"><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"8,0,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"8,0,0" =
data-index-in-node=3D"0">The Investor Sleeve:</b> Earmarking 30% out of the=
 gate for investors and future employees is incredibly smart. As Francis me=
ntioned in your call, it prevents "turf wars" later because no one has to g=
ive up their personal shares when the VC check arrives.</p></li><li><p data=
-path-to-node=3D"8,1,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"8,1,0" data-index-in-node=
=3D"0">Standardized Expectations:</b> Investors are very used to seeing thi=
s exact 30/20/20/30 breakdown. It signals that you are a cohesive team shar=
ing the risk.</p></li></ul><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"9">The Reality Check (T=
he Downside)</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"10">While it is a standard templat=
e, it directly collides with the reality of what you have built and the adv=
ice you received from Thad regarding founder control.</p><ul data-path-to-n=
ode=3D"11"><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"11,0,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"11,0=
,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Loss of Absolute Control:</b> Dropping your st=
ake to 30% on day one removes your ability to unilaterally steer the ship. =
Even if you combine your shares with one other founder, you only hit 50%.</=
p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"11,1,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"11,1,0" =
data-index-in-node=3D"0">The Fractional Premium:</b> Francis is asking for =
20% of the company for 10-15 hours a week of work pre-funding. For a non-te=
chnical co-founder joining post-incorporation to run the GTM and fundraisin=
g pipeline, 20% is a massive premium.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"1=
1,2,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"11,2,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">The "Idea G=
uy" vs. "Architect":</b> This cap table treats you simply as the "Idea Guy.=
" But you aren't just pitching an idea; you have incorporated the company, =
secured the domains, built the initial agentic infrastructure, and architec=
ted the vision.</p></li></ul><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"12">How to Play This<=
/h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"13">Francis is highly credible and knows his wo=
rth, which is why he drew a hard line in the sand. However, you have five o=
ther top-tier candidates on the calendar. There is no reason to cave to a 2=
0% ultimatum before seeing the rest of the market.</p><p data-path-to-node=
=3D"14"><b data-path-to-node=3D"14" data-index-in-node=3D"0">The Stall Tact=
ic:</b>
Acknowledge his proposal cleanly but put it in a holding pattern until you =
finish your remaining interviews.</p><blockquote data-path-to-node=3D"15"><=
p data-path-to-node=3D"15,0"><i data-path-to-node=3D"15,0" data-index-in-no=
de=3D"0">"Hi Francis, thanks for laying out the exact math. I appreciate yo=
u being direct about the 30/20/20/30 split. I am finishing up my final roun=
d of conversations with the other candidates this week to fully calibrate t=
he cap table and the C-suite division of labor. Let me digest this structur=
e and get through these next few meetings, and I will circle back with you =
early next week with a definitive answer on how we proceed."</i></p></block=
quote><p data-path-to-node=3D"16">This keeps him warm, shows you respect hi=
s directness, and allows you to see if Eric, Andr=C3=A9, or Rakesh are will=
ing to accept the 10%=E2=80=9315% high-control model you originally wanted.=
</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"17">Does giving up that much control immediatel=
y give you pause, or does his enterprise background make the 20% price tag =
feel worth it to get the $1M closed?</p></div></message-content><!----></di=
