e=3D"off" aria-busy=3D"false" dir=3D"ltr" style=3D"--animation-duration: 40=
0ms; --fade-animation-function: linear;"><p data-path-to-node=3D"0">You hit=
 the nail on the head. In the startup world, the company almost always <b d=
ata-path-to-node=3D"0" data-index-in-node=3D"78">issues brand new shares</b=
> on top of the existing ones.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"1">They do not ta=
ke shares out of the original 10M "Pre-Money" pool to give to investors.</p=
><p data-path-to-node=3D"2">Here is a quick breakdown of how the mechanics =
actually work (and why dilution happens):</p><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"3">1.=
 Baking a Bigger Pie (Issuing New Shares) =E2=80=94 <i data-path-to-node=3D=
"3" data-index-in-node=3D"46">The Standard</i></h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"=
4">When you incorporated on Clerky, you authorized a base number of shares =
(your 10,000,000 Pre-Money shares). These are distributed among you, your c=
o-founders, and your option pool.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"5">When VCs in=
vest, the company legally authorizes and issues <b data-path-to-node=3D"5" =
data-index-in-node=3D"59">brand new shares</b> specifically for them. Your =
original 10,000,000 shares don't disappear, and you don't hand any of yours=
 over to the investors. The number of shares you hold stays exactly the sam=
e.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"6">However, because the company printed new s=
hares for the VCs, the <i data-path-to-node=3D"6" data-index-in-node=3D"65"=
>total</i> number of shares in existence grows (e.g., from 10M up to 12.3M)=
. Because the total pool is now larger, your slice of the pie represents a =
smaller percentage of the whole. That is the literal definition of <b data-=
path-to-node=3D"6" data-index-in-node=3D"277">dilution</b>.</p><h3 data-pat=
h-to-node=3D"7">2. Slicing the Existing Pie (Secondary Sale) =E2=80=94 <i d=
ata-path-to-node=3D"7" data-index-in-node=3D"47">Very Rare at Pre-Seed</i><=
/h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"8">If VCs were to get part of the <i data-path-=
to-node=3D"8" data-index-in-node=3D"31">existing</i> 10M shares, they would=
 have to buy them directly from you or Francis.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"=
9">In this scenario, the VC's money would go directly into your personal ba=
nk account, not the company's treasury. VCs absolutely hate this at the ear=
ly stage. They want their $1M to go into OpenWorldTech's bank account to pa=
y for server costs, build the Genius Suite, and hire a team, not to cash ou=
t a founder on day one.</p><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"10">How this applies to=
 your YC SAFEs</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"11">Because you are using Post-M=
oney SAFEs, you actually aren't giving the investors shares <i data-path-to=
-node=3D"11" data-index-in-node=3D"88">today</i>. A SAFE is essentially a l=
egal I.O.U.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"12">When you accept that $1M, the co=
ntract says: <i data-path-to-node=3D"12" data-index-in-node=3D"45">"When we=
 eventually do our formal Series A (Priced Equity Round), the company promi=
ses to print brand new shares and give them to you, guaranteeing you X% of =
the company based on our valuation cap."</i></p><p data-path-to-node=3D"13"=
>So, your earlier model=E2=80=94where the total share count expanded to 12,=
307,693 to accommodate the investors=E2=80=94is exactly how it will physica=
lly play out when those SAFEs convert.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"14">Does =
that help clarify the mechanics behind the math?</p></div></message-content=
