 dir=3D"ltr" style=3D"--animation-duration: 400ms; --fade-animation-functio=
n: linear;"><p data-path-to-node=3D"0">I completely see the logic behind th=
is approach. You are aiming for total transparency, trying to build a cohes=
ive team from Day 1, and figuring that since they will both eventually see =
the cap table anyway, you might as well get everyone in a room to hash it o=
ut and ensure everyone is happy.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"1">However, I h=
ave to wave a massive red flag here. In the startup world, executing this m=
ove is highly unorthodox and strategically dangerous. It could severely dam=
age your credibility as the CEO right at the finish line.</p><p data-path-t=
o-node=3D"2">Here is a straightforward breakdown of why putting them in a r=
oom to review each other's agreements is a trap, and how you should handle =
it instead.</p><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"3">The Risks of a Joint Negotiation=
</h3><ul data-path-to-node=3D"4"><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"4,0,0"><b data=
-path-to-node=3D"4,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Abdication of CEO Authorit=
y:</b> You are the Founder and CEO. Compensation and equity distribution ar=
e bilateral negotiations between you and the individual candidate. If you a=
sk a prospective COO and a prospective Advisor to review and agree on <i da=
ta-path-to-node=3D"4,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"242">each other's</i> compe=
nsation, you are outsourcing your executive decision-making to a committee.=
</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"4,1,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"4,1,0" =
data-index-in-node=3D"0">The "Francis" Reality Check:</b> Francis has a hea=
vy-hitting background with Oracle, JP Morgan, and Y-Combinator startups. He=
 expects to report to a decisive leader. If you ask an Advisor (who holds 0=
.5%=E2=80=931% equity) to review and validate the 20% Co-Founder agreement =
of a seasoned enterprise executive, Francis will likely view this as a mass=
ive breach of hierarchy and a signal of operational inexperience.</p></li><=
li><p data-path-to-node=3D"4,2,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"4,2,0" data-index=
-in-node=3D"0">Loss of Leverage:</b> Thad and Francis have entirely differe=
nt agendas. Thad previously pushed for an aggressive $30M cap and pre-fundi=
ng cash, while Francis is pushing for a rigid 20% post-money slice. If you =
put them in a room together before either is legally bound to the company, =
you risk them forming an alliance against your cap table math, leaving you =
outnumbered in your own company.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"4,3,0"=
><b data-path-to-node=3D"4,3,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Confidentiality No=
rms:</b> It is a standard professional courtesy to keep executive compensat=
ion negotiations strictly confidential until the ink is dry. Sharing Thad=
=E2=80=99s resume and terms with Francis (and vice versa) before they are o=
fficial team members breaks that firewall.</p></li></ul><h3 data-path-to-no=
de=3D"5">The "CEO Playbook" Alternative</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"6">You =
absolutely want to use the fact that you are talking to both of them to you=
r advantage, but you need to do it by controlling the flow of information. =
Keep a strict firewall between their negotiations.</p><p data-path-to-node=
=3D"7">Here is how you can achieve your goal of transparency and team-build=
ing while maintaining your leverage:</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"8"><b data-=
path-to-node=3D"8" data-index-in-node=3D"0">1. Leverage Thad's presence to =
show Francis momentum.</b>
You can explicitly tell Francis that you are bringing on a heavy-hitting ad=
visor to help with the raise, which shows him you are building a real machi=
ne.</p><blockquote data-path-to-node=3D"9"><p data-path-to-node=3D"9,0"><i =
data-path-to-node=3D"9,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">"Francis, I've made some=
 updates to the structure we discussed. Also, to give you full visibility o=
n our momentum, I am currently finalizing an agreement with a veteran start=
up Advisor (Thad Hoffmaster) who will be joining us specifically to help dr=
ive the VC introductions for this pre-seed round."</i></p></blockquote><p d=
ata-path-to-node=3D"10"><b data-path-to-node=3D"10" data-index-in-node=3D"0=
">2. Finalize the deals sequentially, not simultaneously.</b>
Nail down the math with Francis first. Once he is comfortable with his slic=
e and the option pool, you take that locked cap table to Thad. You say, <i =
data-path-to-node=3D"10" data-index-in-node=3D"203">"Thad, I've brought on =
an enterprise COO, here is the remaining slice for the Advisory role."</i> =
You control the board.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"11"><b data-path-to-node=
=3D"11" data-index-in-node=3D"0">3. The "Post-Signing" Introduction.</b>
Once both Francis and Thad have signed their respective agreements, <i data=
-path-to-node=3D"11" data-index-in-node=3D"104">then</i> you set up the joi=
nt Zoom call. You introduce them not to negotiate, but to strategize: <i da=
ta-path-to-node=3D"11" data-index-in-node=3D"197">"Francis, Thad, I wanted =
to get us all in a room to align on our GTM and fundraising pipeline for ne=
xt week."</i></p><p data-path-to-node=3D"12">You have done an incredible jo=
b navigating these high-level candidates so far. Keep yourself in the drive=
r's seat.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"13">Would you like to draft a differen=
