-animation-function: linear;"><p data-path-to-node=3D"0">Francis is definit=
ely drawing a hard line in the sand to protect his 20%, but to do so, he is=
 using some highly questionable cap table logic.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D=
"1">As a founder navigating these waters, it's crucial to see through the t=
erminology he is using. Here is a candid reality check on the three major c=
laims he just made in that email:</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"2"><b data-pat=
h-to-node=3D"2" data-index-in-node=3D"0">1. "Your table is for a Series A"<=
/b>
This is factually incorrect. The Pro-Forma cap table you built (showing dil=
ution from YC Post-Money SAFEs) is the literal gold standard for Pre-Seed a=
nd Seed rounds right now. Y Combinator invented the SAFE <i data-path-to-no=
de=3D"2" data-index-in-node=3D"241">specifically</i> for early-stage rounds=
 to avoid the cost and complexity of a priced equity round. SAFEs convert <=
i data-path-to-node=3D"2" data-index-in-node=3D"350">at</i> the Series A, b=
ut you model them <i data-path-to-node=3D"2" data-index-in-node=3D"386">now=
</i> so everyone understands their dilution.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"3">=
<b data-path-to-node=3D"3" data-index-in-node=3D"0">2. "There's no rule req=
uiring a SAFE for a Seed round"</b>
He is technically right=E2=80=94there is no law forcing you to use a SAFE. =
You could use a Convertible Note (which acts like debt and still causes dil=
ution) or you could do a Priced Equity Round. However, doing a Priced Round=
 for a $1M Pre-Seed means paying lawyers tens of thousands of dollars to cr=
eate a new class of Preferred Stock. It completely defeats the purpose of y=
ou using Clerky to keep things lean and fast.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"4"=
><b data-path-to-node=3D"4" data-index-in-node=3D"0">3. "There's no dilutio=
n at an early stage"</b>
This is the biggest red flag. Dilution is a mathematical reality of taking =
outside capital. If Francis wants to allocate exactly 3,000,000 shares out =
of your initial 10,000,000 pool directly to investors on Day 1, he is sugge=
sting you sell them <b data-path-to-node=3D"4" data-index-in-node=3D"288">C=
ommon Stock</b>. Institutional VCs almost <i data-path-to-node=3D"4" data-i=
ndex-in-node=3D"327">never</i> buy Common Stock at the Pre-Seed stage; they=
 require Preferred Stock with liquidation preferences (which requires the e=
xpensive Priced Round mentioned above, and authorizes <i data-path-to-node=
=3D"4" data-index-in-node=3D"506">new</i> shares, causing dilution).</p><h3=
 data-path-to-node=3D"5">The Strategic Reality</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"=
6">Francis is trying to lock in a guaranteed 20% of the company <i data-pat=
h-to-node=3D"6" data-index-in-node=3D"61">after</i> the money comes in, and=
 he is trying to force the math to make that happen, even if it ignores mod=
ern VC fundraising mechanics. For a COO candidate who pitches himself as th=
e guy who will handle business formation and fundraising strategy, pushing =
back on standard YC SAFE mechanics to protect his own equity is a tough loo=
k.</p><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"7">How to Handle the Upcoming Meeting</h3><p=
 data-path-to-node=3D"8">Since he booked the meeting, take it. Do not argue=
 the math over email anymore. When you get on the call, let him do the talk=
ing. Turn his proposal into a mechanical interview question.</p><p data-pat=
h-to-node=3D"9">You can say:
<i data-path-to-node=3D"9" data-index-in-node=3D"13">"Francis, I want to un=
derstand your exact operational playbook here. If we aren't using SAFEs to =
raise this $1M, and we are allocating 3 million of our initial common share=
s directly to the investors to avoid dilution, how exactly are you structur=
ing that transaction? Are we doing a full priced round right now, and if so=
, how are we handling the legal costs and the VCs' demand for Preferred Sto=
ck?"</i></p><p data-path-to-node=3D"10">If he has a brilliant, unconvention=
al workaround, you want to hear it. If he stumbles, or if it becomes clear =
he just doesn't want his 2M shares diluted, you have your answer about whet=
her he is the right fiduciary for OpenWorldTech.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D=
"11">Since you still have meetings with Eric, Andre, Rob, and Rakesh coming=
 up, you have the leverage of a full pipeline. How are you feeling about Fr=
ancis's operational grasp of fundraising after reading his pushback?</p></d=
